[5.805488, "o", "cat /app/task/task.toml\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[5.806234, "o", "schema_version = \"1.4\"\r\n\r\n[task]\r\nname        = \"dfir-eval/log4shell-webshell\"\r\nversion     = \"1.0.0\"\r\ndescription = \"Post-compromise forensic investigation: Ubuntu web host breached via Log4Shell, webshell + reverse shell, cron/systemd persistence, timestomping.\"\r\nauthors     = [{ name = \"DFIR Synthetic Corpus\" }]\r\nkeywords    = [\"security\", \"dfir\", \"incident-response\", \"long-horizon\"]\r\n\r\n[metadata]\r\nauthor_name  = \"DFIR Synthetic Corpus\"\r\nauthor_email = \"\"\r\ndifficulty   = \"hard\"\r\ncategory     = \"security\"\r\ntags         = [\"security\", \"dfir\", \"incident-response\", \"long-horizon\"]\r\n\r\n[agent]\r\ntimeout_sec = 3600.0\r\n\r\n[verifier]\r\ntimeout_sec = 900.0\r\n\r\n[verifier.env]\r\n# Tier-3 LLM judge (OPTIONAL). ${VAR:-} = empty default, so a missing var does NOT block the\r\n# run (Harbor would treat a bare ${VAR} as required) — the judge degrades to Tier 1+2. To\r\n# enable it: export REWARDKIT_JUDGE=anthropic/claude-opus-5 and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY before running.\r\nANTHROPIC_API_KEY = \"${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}\"\r\nREWARDKIT_JUDGE   = \"${REWARDKIT_JUDGE:-}\"\r\n\r\n[environment]\r\n# Analysis machine is Linux regardless of the victim OS in the forensic image at /disk.\r\nos                = \"linux\"\r\nbuild_timeout_sec = 900.0\r\n"]
[5.806873, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[5.807067, "o", "root@98ca416e1de0:/app# "]
[6.796322, "i", "cat /app/task/instruction.md\n"]
[6.797921, "o", "cat /app/task/instruction.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r# Post-Compromise Forensic Investigation and Remediation Planning\r\n\r\nYou are a DFIR analyst working on an **analysis machine** — **not** the compromised server.\r\nA read-only forensic copy of the host is mounted at `/disk`, and the output of the\r\nsecurity product (alerts, vulnerability scan, baseline checks) is at `/sas`.\r\n\r\n## Scenario\r\n\r\nAn internet-facing Ubuntu 22.04 application server was exploited through a Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228) request against a vulnerable logging path. The attacker dropped a JSP webshell, established a reverse shell, installed cron and systemd persistence, and timestomped the webshell to blend in. The alert feed surfaces the exploit and the systemd unit; the webshell's cron twin and the tool residue are silent.\r\n\r\n## Rules (load-bearing — do not violate)\r\n\r\n- You are on an analysis machine, not the compromised host.\r\n- `/disk` is a **read-only** forensic copy. Do not attempt to modify it.\r\n- Do **not** SSH into any machine or execute remediation — you are producing a plan, not\r\n  running it.\r\n- Every finding must be based on evidence in `/disk` or `/sas`.\r\n- Do **not** fabricate CVEs. State confidence levels when unsure.\r\n- In the remediation plan, prefer backup/quarantine over deletion.\r\n\r\n## Evidence is messy and multi-format\r\n\r\nReal disks are not clean text. Evidence is spread across log files, CSV/TSV tables, JSON\r\ntelemetry, config files, registry hives, `.evtx` event logs, inert binary samples, and\r\nmarkup — some of it rotated, gzipped, truncated at a cleared window, or partially\r\noverwritten. Use the right tool: `grep`/`rg`, `strings` (and `strings -e l` for UTF-16 in\r\nhives/event logs), `zcat` for `*.gz`, `jq` for JSON, `xxd` for raw bytes, `pdftotext` for\r\nPDFs. Indicators of compromise in provided intel are **defanged** (`hxxp://`, `evil[.]com`,\r\n`stratum+tcp[:]//`) — re-fang them mentally when correlating.\r\n\r\nBesides `/disk` (the host image) and the machine-readable feeds in `/sas`,\r\n`/sas` also carries analyst materials: `/sas/cti/` — a vendor threat advisory\r\n(text, PDF, and page-image PNGs) mapping the campaign to MITRE ATT&CK techniques and any\r\nrelevant CVEs; and `/sas/handoff/` — a **pre-filled change ticket with stale/incorrect\r\nentries to reconcile in place**, a findings JSON schema, and an incident intake form. Map your\r\non-disk findings to the advisory's ATT&CK claims, and correct the change ticket against your\r\nown evidence rather than trusting it.\r\n\r\n## Citations (required)\r\n\r\nEvery finding in every report must be followed by a **backtick-delimited** citation of the\r\nform `` `/absolute/path` `` or `` `/absolute/path:line` `` pointing at evidence under\r\n`/disk` **or** `/sas`. Use `/sas` for findings whose only evidence is\r\nan alert — e.g. a network connection, or an event whose primary log was wiped. **Findings\r\nwithout a resolvable citation will not be credited.**\r\n\r\n## Deliverables\r\n\r\nWrite all five files to `/app`. A run that produces fewer than five is scored as\r\nincomplete, not as a low score.\r\n\r\n| File | Content |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `progress.md` | Investigation timeline and working checklist |\r\n| `intrusion-report.md` | Attack chain, evidence, entities, persistence, impact |\r\n| `vuln-report.md` | Vulnerability-driven entry analysis (or its explicit absence) |\r\n| `baseline-report.md` | Baseline / configuration weaknesses |\r\n| `remediation-plan.md` | Prioritised, correct, complete, verifiable plan |\r\n\r\nDetection and remediation are scored on **separate** axes. Investigate the whole host — the\r\nalerts do not cover everything that happened.\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@98ca416e1de0:/app# "]
[12.119314, "i", "cat /app/task/environment/Dockerfile\n"]
[12.119839, "o", "cat /app/task/environment/Dockerfile\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[12.120256, "o", "FROM ubuntu:24.04\r\nENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\r\n\r\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\r\n      file binutils grep ripgrep jq python3 python3-pip less tree \\\r\n      gzip xxd poppler-utils ca-certificates \\\r\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\r\n# binutils -> strings/strings -e l (binary blobs, registry hives, .evtx); gzip -> zcat rotated\r\n# logs; xxd -> hexdump; poppler-utils -> pdftotext (CTI PDFs); ca-certificates -> judge HTTPS.\r\n\r\n# Read-only forensic image at /disk (extracted INTO /disk, not /), security-product feed at /sas.\r\nCOPY disk.tar.gz /tmp/\r\nCOPY disk.tar.gz.sha256 /tmp/\r\nRUN cd /tmp && sha256sum -c disk.tar.gz.sha256 \\\r\n && mkdir -p /disk && tar -xzf disk.tar.gz -C /disk \\\r\n && chmod -R a-w /disk && rm /tmp/disk.tar.gz*\r\n\r\nCOPY sas-mock /sas\r\nRUN chmod -R a-w /sas\r\nWORKDIR /app\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@98ca416e1de0:/app# "]
[13.110879, "i", "cat /app/task/tests/test.sh\n"]
[13.111139, "o", "cat /app/task/tests/test.sh\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[13.111862, "o", "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r\n# tests/test.sh  (FIXED — entry point Harbor calls to score a run)\r\n# Scores /app against this range's manifest using the vendored rewardkit runtime. The runtime\r\n# and criterion code are identical for every range in the subdomain; only manifest.json\r\n# differs (doc 02 §3.0).\r\n# NOTE: no `set -e` — we must ALWAYS write the reward file, even if the runtime errors.\r\nset -uo pipefail\r\n\r\n: \"${REWARDKIT_JUDGE:=}\"   # optional Tier-3 judge (provider/model); Tier 1+2 run without it\r\n\r\nhere=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"          # the /tests directory (copied in by Harbor)\r\nexport PYTHONPATH=\"$here:${PYTHONPATH:-}\"       # so `rewardkit` and `criteria` import\r\napp=\"${RK_APP:-/app}\"                           # the agent's workspace (deliverables)\r\nout=\"${RK_OUT:-/logs/verifier}\"                 # Harbor reads the reward from here\r\nmkdir -p \"$out\"\r\n\r\npython3 -m rewardkit --tests \"$here\" --app \"$app\" --disk \"${RK_DISK:-/disk}\" --sas \"${RK_SAS:-/sas}\" || true\r\n\r\n# Harbor reads the numeric reward (float in [0,1]) from /logs/verifier/reward.txt. Always\r\n# (re)write it — never trust a pre-existing file the agent may have dropped in /app.\r\nreward=\"$(python3 -c \"import os,json; p='$app/reward.json'; print(json.load(open(p))['reward'] if os.path.exists(p) else 0.0)\" 2>/dev/null || echo 0.0)\"\r\n[ -z \"$reward\" ] && reward=0.0\r\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$reward\" > \"$out/reward.txt\"\r\n# IMPORTANT: the filename `reward.json` in /logs/verifier is RESERVED by Harbor — if present it\r\n# is preferred over reward.txt and parsed as dict[str,float|int], so EVERY value must be numeric.\r\n# The runtime's /app/reward.json is a human diagnostic that carries non-numeric fields (the\r\n# `outcome` status string, and a null `quality` when the Tier-3 judge is off) — copying it here\r\n# verbatim makes Harbor raise a ValidationError. Preserve it under a non-reserved name instead.\r\n[ -f \"$app/reward.json\" ] && cp \"$app/reward.json\" \"$out/reward-full.json\" 2>/dev/null || true\r\necho \"reward=$reward\"\r\nexit 0\r\n"]
